1
00:00:01,792 --> 00:00:05,393
Earth has about 370
quintillion gallons of water.

2
00:00:05,460 --> 00:00:11,213
If all that water was inside a single
drop, it'd be 860 miles wide.

3
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,313
Of course, it's not.

4
00:00:13,380 --> 00:00:16,433
Earth's water can be
found all over the planet.

5
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:21,233
Earth's water budget tells us where
that water is and what form it takes.

6
00:00:21,300 --> 00:00:23,813
Just like you might have
some money in your wallet,

7
00:00:23,880 --> 00:00:27,533
some saved in the bank or some
in a long term investment,

8
00:00:27,600 --> 00:00:31,733
Earth's water is spread across
lakes, rivers, glaciers,

9
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:33,773
oceans and groundwater.

10
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:38,198
With some being easier to access
and use and some, well,

11
00:00:38,265 --> 00:00:39,683
not so much.

12
00:00:39,750 --> 00:00:43,313
So, where is all 370
quintillion gallons of water?

13
00:00:43,380 --> 00:00:48,263
And how much can we use for drinking,
growing food and daily life?

14
00:00:48,330 --> 00:00:52,517
If you've seen a picture of Earth from space
or if you're lucky enough to have been

15
00:00:52,584 --> 00:00:55,553
to space, it'll be no surprise
that most of Earth's water,

16
00:00:55,620 --> 00:01:00,173
a whopping 96.5%
of it is in Earth's oceans.

17
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:05,316
While this salty water provides a great
habitat for all sorts of marine life and

18
00:01:05,383 --> 00:01:10,934
makes a nice setting for surfing a wave,
the water in Earth's oceans isn't

19
00:01:11,001 --> 00:01:11,501
drinkable.

20
00:01:11,520 --> 00:01:17,526
That means just 3.5% of Earth's water
is fresh and might be used for drinking or

21
00:01:17,593 --> 00:01:18,173
watering crops.

22
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:20,753
Might is the important word here.

23
00:01:20,820 --> 00:01:24,413
Nearly half of Earth's freshwater
is frozen solid in glaciers,

24
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:29,933
ice caps and permanent snow pack, making
it pretty tough to access as usable water.

25
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,983
The next most abundant source of
freshwater is what's known as groundwater.

26
00:01:34,050 --> 00:01:37,575
As water seeps into the ground
from above, it fills the cracks,

27
00:01:37,642 --> 00:01:39,923
pores and spaces between bits of rock.

28
00:01:39,990 --> 00:01:43,973
If you look deep enough, you might find
that all the space between rock particles

29
00:01:44,040 --> 00:01:46,553
has been filled, forming an aquifer.

30
00:01:47,040 --> 00:01:51,308
Aquifers store huge amounts of water
that can be pumped out to the surface for

31
00:01:51,375 --> 00:01:53,333
drinking water and irrigating crops.

32
00:01:53,400 --> 00:01:58,633
Like a savings account, it can take a long
time, even centuries for groundwater to

33
00:01:58,700 --> 00:01:59,200
accumulate.

34
00:01:59,220 --> 00:02:04,613
That leaves just 0.1% of all water that
isn't salty ocean water or fresh water

35
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:06,833
that's frozen or buried in the ground.

36
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:09,773
If you are looking for easy
access to what remains,

37
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:15,593
think again, most of this tiny fraction
of Earth's water is frozen as permafrost,

38
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:21,128
some exists as moisture in the soil, a bit
is in the atmosphere as water vapor and

39
00:02:21,195 --> 00:02:24,743
some makes up the water that exists
within living creatures.

40
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:30,064
Just 0.01% of all water on Earth
is on the surface in lakes,

41
00:02:30,131 --> 00:02:33,113
rivers, swamps and marshes.

42
00:02:33,660 --> 00:02:38,033
Most water on Earth has been here for
billions of years and during that time,

43
00:02:38,100 --> 00:02:42,263
any single drop might have moved between
different parts of Earth's water budget.

44
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:46,802
Moving from one part of the budget to another,
a process known as the water cycle

45
00:02:46,869 --> 00:02:51,197
can happen quickly, like when water
evaporates from a lake and moves to the

46
00:02:51,264 --> 00:02:51,764
atmosphere.

47
00:02:51,630 --> 00:02:56,513
It can also happen slowly, like when
water makes its way deep underground.

48
00:02:56,580 --> 00:03:00,976
Understanding where these different forms
of water exist and how they move is an

49
00:03:01,043 --> 00:03:04,973
important part of understanding our planet
and planning for future water needs.

50
00:03:05,040 --> 00:03:09,653
NASA has a fleet of satellites studying
the location and movement of Earth's water,

51
00:03:09,720 --> 00:03:12,503
each designed to measure
certain types of water.

52
00:03:12,570 --> 00:03:15,413
Some measure the movement
of groundwater ocean currents,

53
00:03:15,480 --> 00:03:20,423
while new missions like SWOT and NISAR
will also fill gaps in our knowledge by

54
00:03:20,490 --> 00:03:22,943
measuring fresh water
and possible hazards.

55
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:28,283
Together, these missions help bring
NASA's water budget and cycle into focus.

56
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:32,663
So next time you feel a drop of rain on your face,
imagine all the places that water

57
00:03:32,730 --> 00:03:38,693
might have been during the last few billion
years and where it might be in the next.

58
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:42,123
(upbeat music)

